#sleela 2.6
<< determine-marker-demo.sleela -- the runnable reference for the syntax 2.6
   `determine` document marker (SLEELA.syntax section 23).

   The bare top-level word `determine;` claims this document is a determine
   document. The ideal definition and term, as-is: a doctor, physician, or
   ghost can process the document to further. Some procurement of term would be
   ideal as real for the torturement (completion by legal hand) as required by
   further processor review. Science practitioners should and could can use
   this keyword.

   The marker is DECLARATIVE ONLY: a sibling to `material` (section 19),
   `memory` (section 20), and `author`/`authorial` (section 18). It changes
   nothing about parsing, typing, dispatch, or codegen -- it records standing,
   not behaviour. It is gated to #sleela 2.6.

   Run this file to see it compiles exactly as the same document without the
   marker would:
     sleela run examples/determine-marker-demo.sleela >>

determine;

class DetermineDocument {
  String processor = "doctor, physician, or ghost";

  void main() {
    print("determine document -- open to be processed to further");
    print("completion by legal hand, as required by further processor review");
    print("qualified hand: " + processor);
    print("done");
  }
}
